Ansible Playbooks Basics

Today I learned about Ansible Playbooks, which are the backbone of structured automation.
If Ad-Hoc commands are quick actions ⚡
 Playbooks are complete automation plans 📘
Think of them as a step-by-step execution blueprint.

🔹 📘 Playbook Structure
A Playbook is written in YAML and contains:
• Plays
 • Tasks
 • Target hosts
 • Modules

Basic structure:

- name: Install nginx
 hosts: webservers
 become: yes
 tasks:
 - name: Install package
 apt:
 name: nginx
 state: present

Each section clearly defines what action will run and where.

🔹 🎯 Plays & Tasks
✔ Play → Defines which machines to target
 ✔ Tasks → Define the actions to execute

A single play can contain multiple tasks.
 Tasks always run in sequence (top to bottom) 🔄

🔹 🌐 hosts & become
🔸 hosts → Specifies target server or group
 Example: hosts: webservers
🔸 become → Enables privilege escalation (sudo)
 Example: become: yes
This ensures tasks run with required permissions 🔐

🔹 ▶ Running Playbooks

To execute a playbook:
ansible-playbook playbook.yml

With custom inventory:
ansible-playbook -i inventory playbook.yml

Playbooks are reusable, scalable, and production-ready 🚀

🔥 3-Line Takeaway
Playbooks bring structure to automation.
 Plays define where, tasks define what.
 Automation becomes powerful when it’s reusable.
